cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this repository, cite the paper below and the prior work it credits (paper, Section
  1.3 and references).
title: 'Certified exact minima of Kochen-Specker sets inside coordinate pools: 31 and 33 rays in dimension
  three, 18 in dimension four, 29 in dimension five'
type: software
authors:
- family-names: Donahue
  given-names: Alexander S.
  orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0006-8168-9648
  alias: 0thernes
  affiliation: 0thernes LLC, Washington, USA
  email: 0_0@0thernes.art
  country: US
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-10
repository-code: https://github.com/0thernes/kochen-specker-extremal-lab
url: https://0thernes.github.io/kochen-specker-extremal-lab/
abstract: >-
  A Kochen-Specker (KS) set is a finite set of rays admitting no assignment of 0 and 1 that gives
  exactly one 1 to every orthogonal basis and never 1 to two orthogonal rays. This work determines the
  minimum size of such a set inside prescribed finite pools of rays. Every KS set must contain a basis
  missed by any set of pairwise non-orthogonal rays, which gives a covering problem whose constraints
  are trivial to verify; necessary conditions for critical KS sets, a symmetry normal form and a case
  analysis make it tractable in pools of up to 651 rays. Three independent exact methods are used and
  each result carries a certificate. Results. (1) Among rays of dimension three with integer
  coordinates of absolute value at most 2, 3 or 4 the minimum is 31, and the minimum sets are exactly
  the six images of one Conway-Kochen-type set under the symmetries of the cube. (2) Among rays with
  coordinates 0 or N-th roots of unity, N = 6, 12, 18, 24, the minimum is 33, unique up to symmetry
  for N = 6 and 12. (3) Among the 40 rays of dimension four with coordinates 0, +1, -1 the minimum is
  18, attained only by the 16 known sets inside the 24 rays of Peres. (4) Among the 121 rays of
  dimension five with coordinates 0, +1, -1 the minimum is 29, the size of the set of Cabello,
  Estebaranz and Garcia-Alcaine. The values in (1), the value for N = 6 in (2), and 29 as the smallest
  set generated from the coordinates of (4) had been reported without proof. The minimum values in (1)
  for bound 2 and in (3) are proved in Lean 4 without Mathlib and checked by its kernel. The remaining
  statements rest on unsatisfiability proofs checked by a separate proof checker; the status of every check is recorded in results/RESULTS.md.
  Randomised searches in further pools in dimensions three to eight find nothing smaller than the
  published sets. The general minimal-KS problem is not settled by any of this.
keywords:
- Kochen-Specker theorem
- Kochen-Specker set
- quantum contextuality
- exact minimum
- SAT solving
- DRUP proof
- certificate
- Lean 4
- formal verification
